  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\u27e8x,y\u27e9=4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ k \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\u27e8\\cdot ,\\cdot \u27e9$ in $V,$ then $k=$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8x,y\u27e9 & =4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot ((-4)\\cdot (-3))+k\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =48+2k+0 \\\\  & =48+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\u27e8x,y\u27e9=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8x,y\u27e9 & =0 \\\\ 48+2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =-48 \\\\ k & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$",
